  Hey guys.

  I have been messing around a LOT lately with GRUB and its new features.
It seems that modules can be written for it that get plugged in via menus
and such at boot time.

  I think it would be neat to make a console session of NetHack be
available during a boot OS selection session!

 Sounds *way* better than gabbing and eating doughnuts while fat
fingering the keyboard with sugary fingers... Oh...  wait...  :-)
(I gab at my nethack sessions and eat sugary foods all the time)

  Look, Ma!  No Lard!

  Anyway, it seems like not much processing would be needed.  Likely grab
one of the emu code bodies over under mame and make the module from that.

  Grub allows for these modules to be made, so that they can make their
boot loader more dynamic and portable.

  I see them as pre-boot-diversion capable 'platforms', and I just *know*
you guys always like a new platform!  :-)

  The Grub-Bootloader-Nethack Project(I almost wrote "boatloader",
something a drunk that lives in-a-van-down-by-the-river would say)

  Anytime one sees the boss coming, just boot up!

  Also, far less vulnerable..  Guess what?  Net hooks are available too!

  You guys should be all gobblin' this shit up and things!

  A play platform without an OS! (per se).

 So what is the best base level platform Nethack is written to?  DOS?
Is there a smaller "NetHack Kernel" on another platform?  It would
probably just be easier to emulate the Linux version though.  Are PDA
versions complete game engines?  I can construct an 80 x 25 *inside* my
1920x1080 Vesa boot screen.  Hell, maybe even 160 x 50 and spruce up the
resolution.  Could write a more complex dungeon generation code with a
bigger array too.  That's a different code edit session though (complete
re-write more like).


  Any thoughts?



